1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
just crosses their arms and says,

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,600
no way, enough is enough,

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,100
I refuse to finish my senior year.

4
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,360
Do you wanna hire a person that stubborn,

5
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,880
that big headed, that defiant?

6
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,080
I don't.

7
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:14,760
They're great friends, I love people like that,

8
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:17,660
they're fun, but I'm not giving them my money.

9
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:20,200
Yeah, Ash?

10
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:21,040
Yeah.

11
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:23,560
Is this, would this then be the only case

12
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,480
where a sunk cost wouldn't be a fallacy?

13
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:27,960
Well, so this is not a sunk cost,

14
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:31,640
this is one where the benefit is unusually high over time.

15
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:33,720
So, here's the thing is,

16
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:35,960
it does make sense to get to a movie,

17
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:37,240
think that it's not as good as you hoped,

18
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:38,600
and then not leave.

19
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:41,800
Because the marginal cost to you

20
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:45,240
of finishing the movie is actually smaller

21
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:46,320
once you're already there.

22
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:47,720
You've already paid the commuting cost,

23
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:52,200
you've already gone and paid the price.

24
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:54,600
So it might be you only need $2 worth of surplus

25
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:55,920
to justify staying in a movie

26
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:58,160
once you've already shown up there.

27
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:01,440
So similarly, here, as long as the value of your time

28
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:03,480
isn't 70, of one year,

29
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:05,480
isn't the discount of present value

30
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:09,560
of 70% of the payoff of college for the rest of your life,

31
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:13,440
then it would make sense to finish.

32
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:14,320
Now, assuming in general,

33
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:17,200
suppose that every year is equally costly,

34
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,000
but the first year you get $1,

35
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:19,840
second year you get $1,

36
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:20,660
third year you get $1,

37
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,360
and last year you get $7.

38
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,080
It makes a lot of sense to go,

39
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:25,880
and once you've already gotten to the last year

40
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:28,640
stick it out because the payoff is unusually large.

41
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:30,280
Yeah, you have a question there?

42
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:32,040
Are there, I'm just trying to think

43
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:33,360
about possible exceptions,

44
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,040
and I was wondering like, you know,

45
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:36,920
a nursing school or a-

46
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:37,760
Yeah, absolutely.

47
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:40,880
So like there are plenty of majors that earn more,

48
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:43,200
and in general, higher earning majors

49
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,120
do look more vocational.

50
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:46,520
Yeah, but not just that,

51
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,200
it seems like a nursing major,

52
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:53,000
I mean, yes, there's some costly signaling involved

53
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,200
in one's engineering degree.

54
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:56,760
There are actually a few times

55
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:59,600
when you might use something in class in your real life.

56
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:01,240
Yes, so the might is key,

57
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:02,520
but here's the thing is,

58
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:04,960
in general people often think that if a subject is hard

59
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:05,960
and they don't know about it,

60
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:07,480
that it's all human capital,

61
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,160
and they think signaling is just important

62
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:11,400
in all the fields they personally know,

63
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:14,760
but I found that whenever I talk to anyone in any subject,

64
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:16,480
no matter how technical it is,

65
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:18,240
no matter how vocational it is,

66
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:20,320
they always will say they had to jump through

67
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:21,600
a bunch of meaningless hoops

68
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:23,600
that were irrelevant to the job.

69
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:24,800
I'm not saying that's not true,

70
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,840
I'm saying, you know,

71
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:30,440
if the meaningless hoops to the actual information

72
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:35,440
in art history is maybe 99.99.01,

73
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:38,000
in nursing, if you had 10% of useful information.

74
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,480
Yeah, sure, totally believable,

75
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:43,360
and I'd say my only reason for doubting is that,

76
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:46,120
so engineering is a higher absolute return,

77
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:48,480
so it could be the signaling percentage is lower,

78
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:50,080
it could be the signaling percentage is the same,

79
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,480
but the human capital part is just more valuable.

80
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:55,520
Like when they are teaching skills,

81
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,480
they're much more valuable skills.

82
00:02:57,480 --> 00:02:58,320
So I mean, I know that,

83
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,320
so my dad's a PhD in electrical engineering,

84
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:02,560
he said the main thing they did was proofs,

85
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:05,560
which are never used on the job by a practicing engineer.

86
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:06,520
I mean, not surprising,

87
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,560
like who teaches engineering PhDs?

88
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,520
Yes, professors of engineering,

89
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:13,520
and the professors of engineering don't generally,

90
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:14,800
they have some connection to industry,

91
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,840
but most of them are just professors

92
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:18,560
who are like other professors,

93
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:20,240
they like talking about abstract subjects

94
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:22,200
of no practical interest to anyone.

95
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:23,960
My dad never stops telling the story

96
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:26,120
about a seminar he attended in grad school

97
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:28,400
about the design of a non-realizable engine.

98
00:03:29,280 --> 00:03:30,920
And my dad is a super practical guy,

99
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:32,640
stupid, non-realizable engine,

100
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:34,040
why in God are you figuring out

101
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:36,000
how to design a non-realizable engine?

102
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,240
And I'm saying, well, as a professor,

103
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:39,280
that sounds really interesting,

104
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,040
maybe there's some interesting lesson

105
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,120
that you can learn by studying the non-realizable ones,

106
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:45,280
and maybe it teaches you fundamental principles.

107
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:46,360
Ah, bah!

108
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:47,200
Right?

109
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:48,040
Right?

110
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:48,880
And it's all right, well fine,

111
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:51,600
maybe it is of no use to actually designing any machine,

112
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,200
but it's still important and intellectually interesting.

113
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:56,040
Right?

114
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:58,400
And that's not, in the business world,

115
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,840
not considered very important.

116
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,280
So I'm curious to know how widely accepted

117
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,840
these sorts of concepts and this idea,

118
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:07,280
especially the signaling idea,

119
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,360
is being accepted, not just within economics,

120
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:11,680
but within the education establishment,

121
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:15,160
and education scholarship, and among education PhDs.

122
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:16,200
And if they-

123
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:18,280
Which idea, signaling or human capital?

124
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:19,920
As far as the signaling idea,

125
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:21,800
the fact that we're basically getting a certification

126
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:23,680
or a license of some sort.

127
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:25,320
Yeah, so what I'd say is,

128
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:27,320
economists, strangely, are probably the most hostile

129
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:28,440
to the signaling model.

130
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:29,520
So not economists in general,

131
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:32,480
but specialists in labor and education economics

132
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:35,000
are generally very hostile to signaling.

133
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,600
In fields like education, sociology, psychology,

134
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,200
they're much more open to it.

135
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:40,600
Although what they're not open to

136
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:42,320
is drawing the inference that then maybe

137
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:43,240
we don't want to give everyone

138
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:45,040
the best education in the world.

139
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:48,080
Maybe we've actually created a horrible prisoner, Selena,

140
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:49,640
and we should cut back.

141
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:51,480
So that's sort of the policy implication

142
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:53,960
is very hard for non-economists to accept.

143
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:55,240
Economists are really quick to say,

144
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:56,400
well, sure, if we're signaling,

145
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:57,960
then yeah, we're really,

146
00:04:57,960 --> 00:04:59,840
well, government is going and pouring fuel

147
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:01,000
in the fire of waste.

148
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:05,600
But then most economists can see backwards induction

149
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:08,440
and realize they'd have to embrace a very personally

150
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:10,520
and politically unpalatable conclusion.

151
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,920
And so then they, at least my uncharitable theory,

152
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:13,840
is they then say, oh my God,

153
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:15,800
well, we can't accept a theory

154
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:18,640
that would logically imply something so horrifying.

155
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,040
So there's that.

156
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,320
And also based on what you were saying

157
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:25,200
about how little is retained from one year to the next,

158
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:27,200
when you're looking at the growth literature

159
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:28,960
and with comparisons of countries

160
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:31,520
that have X number of years of education

161
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:32,720
and schooling and whatnot,

162
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:35,320
and I'm sure you might be doing that here in the US,

163
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:38,120
how applicable is that really

164
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:41,560
if we're talking about the US, the number one economy,

165
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:45,640
where you have 20, mid-20 somethings,

166
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:48,000
you don't know what the three branches of government is,

167
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,200
but they've had 12, 16 plus years of schooling.

168
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,400
Can we say that that's really robust

169
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:57,240
or is that gonna start?

170
00:05:57,240 --> 00:06:00,360
Yeah, well, I mean, if you're a diehard human capital fan,

171
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:01,960
you could always just say, look,

172
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:03,120
it's really disappointing

173
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:05,560
that education doesn't improve people more,

174
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:09,160
but the important thing is that it improves you enough

175
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:10,200
to justify higher pay.

176
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:13,320
So saying, look, someone who is half literate,

177
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:15,680
well, not half literate,

178
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:18,640
but someone who can read okay is much more employable

179
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:20,720
than someone who can't read at all.

180
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:22,520
Right, and then that makes sense.

181
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:24,920
And so you might very well be willing to pay 30% more

182
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:27,680
for someone who can read poorly

183
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,680
than for someone who can't read at all.

184
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:33,600
But then again, there's the puzzle

185
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:34,840
of what about all these other subjects

186
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,320
that people get penalized for not knowing?

187
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:38,680
So, so, seems in effect to me

188
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:40,160
is one of the most compelling arguments

189
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:41,520
for the power of signaling.

190
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:44,040
Another one, just as you're alluding to,

191
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:46,400
so there are efforts to go and compare

192
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:48,240
the effect of education on individuals

193
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:50,200
versus the effect on countries.

194
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:51,760
And the big punchline out of this literature

195
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:54,760
is that if you raise an individual's income,

196
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:57,560
by race, if you raise an individual's education by a year,

197
00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:59,880
income goes up by maybe 10%.

198
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:02,640
If you raise a country's income by,

199
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:04,620
the average income in a country by a year

200
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:07,840
only seems to go up by about 2%.

201
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:10,800
So in other words, the effect of education

202
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,360
on individual income seems to be about five times as large

203
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:16,320
as for national income, which makes perfect sense here.

204
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:20,040
It's almost all of it signaling

205
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:22,040
because if one person gets more education,

206
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:23,680
you look better compared to the other people.

207
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:25,080
But if everyone gets more education,

208
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,600
you just need more education to look good.

209
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,440
Now, a reply to this that some people have made

210
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:31,840
is well, look, here's the real problem.

211
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:33,200
Education in a lot of these,

212
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:34,640
a lot of verbal countries is terrible.

213
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:36,240
The teachers don't even show up.

214
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:39,480
So of course, it isn't really helping their growth very much.

215
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:42,200
But that would predict that in countries like India

216
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:44,400
that have these horrible education systems,

217
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:47,320
that the private return to education would also be low.

218
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:49,040
It's not.

219
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:51,040
So in India, where the teachers aren't even showing up,

220
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:52,880
it still looks like an extra year of education

221
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:56,040
is buying you an extra 10% of wages for the rest of your life.

222
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:00,440
But the national effect seems to be very small.

223
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:02,880
Another set of evidence that I really like

224
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:04,320
is there's a whole literature,

225
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:06,040
more in sociology than economics,

226
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:07,620
although some economists can do it,

227
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:12,620
on what's called malemployment,

228
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:16,080
or sometimes called overeducation,

229
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:17,920
or just called credential inflation.

230
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:24,880
And it's the idea that over time,

231
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:27,080
the educational credentials that you need to get

232
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:28,960
a given job have gone up,

233
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:30,720
even though the jobs themselves

234
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:33,020
are no more complicated than they were before.

235
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:35,880
All right, so you can look at this

236
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:37,760
and see that now it is totally common

237
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:40,200
for waiters to have college degrees.

238
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:42,760
All right, so what's going on?

239
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:43,920
Is it, well, it's the fancy restaurants,

240
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:45,360
they wanna have waiters with college degrees

241
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:48,600
who go and deal better with the customers.

242
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:50,320
Do you need four years for that?

243
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:52,840
Like, why couldn't you just do, like,

244
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:55,080
one year of waiter school or something like that,

245
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:57,040
if it's all about just being able to go

246
00:08:57,040 --> 00:08:59,520
and mingle with high-class customers?

247
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:01,120
I mean, it seems much more like

248
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:04,560
now that we have so many people with college degrees,

249
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:06,400
some of them lined up not being able

250
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:07,800
to get a regular college-type job,

251
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:09,240
and so they became waiters.

252
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:12,840
Right, so there's a little literature on this.

253
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:16,040
Oh, yeah, and bottom line is that

254
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:17,880
when people have tried to measure,

255
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:21,560
or disaggregate how much of the rise in education

256
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:24,680
is explained by changing of the jobs that we do

257
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:27,000
into more cognitively demanding jobs,

258
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,320
and how much of it can be explained

259
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:32,400
or accounted by rising education requirements

260
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:35,360
within given jobs, the rough breakdown looks like

261
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:38,360
about 20% of the education rise accounted for

262
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:40,440
with more cognitively demanding jobs,

263
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:44,680
80% the giant remainder just that you now need

264
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:46,400
more education to get a given job.

265
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:48,600
You might need a high school degree

266
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:50,280
to be a garbage man now.

267
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:51,200
Things like this.

268
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:52,440
Now, of course, sometimes you may say,

269
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:53,840
well, maybe it's for government jobs

270
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:55,360
or jobs where there's an education requirement

271
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:56,680
for licensing.

272
00:09:56,680 --> 00:09:58,000
That may be a little bit of it,

273
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,200
but we can actually go and look at all of the industries,

274
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:04,080
like private sector employment or unlicensed industries,

275
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:06,120
and we can still see, even there,

276
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:09,960
it looks like employers are paying substantial premiums

277
00:10:09,960 --> 00:10:11,920
to go and get people with high school degrees

278
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:15,000
or college degrees for jobs that common sense says

279
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,360
are not college jobs,

280
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:19,400
they're not even really high school jobs.

281
00:10:20,680 --> 00:10:23,000
And then there's one other literature,

282
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:24,640
but it's a little complicated, so I won't go into it,

283
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:26,320
but I'll leave something for the imagination

284
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:29,320
in case you wanna take the class.

285
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:32,280
All right, so bottom line is

286
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:33,360
the signaling model for education

287
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:34,200
seems to make a lot of sense.

288
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:35,040
Oh yeah, one other thing.

289
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:37,040
So I also have a bunch of appeals

290
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:38,800
to firsthand experience that I like.

291
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:40,200
So I've already kind of done one,

292
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:43,120
which is why is it that you worry about

293
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:46,040
failing a class, but you don't worry about

294
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:48,800
forgetting the material in the class after the final exam?

295
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:51,920
In human capital terms, that's pretty odd,

296
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:54,280
because from the point of view of employers,

297
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:55,520
there's no difference between someone

298
00:10:55,520 --> 00:10:58,280
who forgot the material and someone who never knew it.

299
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:01,400
But in signaling terms, there's a big difference,

300
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:04,560
because if you did a class that you didn't really like,

301
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:07,040
got an A, and then forgot all the material,

302
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:09,440
you still prove something about yourself.

303
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:11,480
And as long as willingness to submit

304
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:15,280
to boring, pointless tasks is persistent throughout life,

305
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:16,880
the fact that this one person went

306
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:18,400
and did a bunch of these things before

307
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:19,680
and then forgot the material

308
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:21,000
still makes them more employable

309
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:21,840
than someone who just said,

310
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:24,680
"'Screw you, I don't wanna do that stuff, it's stupid.'"

311
00:11:24,680 --> 00:11:25,520
Yeah?

312
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:28,360
If you've learned something once and you forget it,

313
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:29,360
you can learn it faster.

314
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:31,640
That's very true, and there's plenty of literature on that,

315
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:34,040
but what if you don't even use it on the job?

316
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:36,800
Yeah, so why is it that you don't worry about that?

317
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:37,920
It's like, I could always learn it again

318
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:39,320
if I ever need to know it.

319
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:40,800
Of course, I'm almost certainly never gonna need

320
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:42,320
to know it again, unless maybe one day

321
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,040
you're teaching your kid it's the same material.

322
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:46,320
It's like, oh my God, trigonometry.

323
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:47,800
Now I better teach my kids how to do it.

324
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,480
Just saying, well, how did it go again?

325
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:52,000
Yeah, so there's that one.

326
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,840
Another one that I like is that actually,

327
00:11:57,880 --> 00:11:58,720
so let me do it this way.

328
00:11:58,720 --> 00:11:59,560
So you might be signaling

329
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:01,720
if you bother to enroll or pay tuition.

330
00:12:02,840 --> 00:12:04,200
Now what do I mean by that?

331
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:08,240
Well, if you go and try to go to a doctor

332
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:09,960
and you show them your health insurance card,

333
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:12,760
normally they want a second document from you right now.

334
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:13,880
What document is that?

335
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:17,160
They wanna see your ID.

336
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:19,040
Why does the doctor wanna see your ID?

337
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:21,120
It's the word you might be trying

338
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:22,800
to get some free healthcare.

339
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:24,000
All right, on the other hand,

340
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,920
have you ever been carded at a college class?

341
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:28,680
Has anyone ever tried to make sure

342
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:30,160
that someone isn't learning for free?

343
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:31,000
Never.

344
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Never, right?

345
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:35,280
So why don't we have to do this?

346
00:12:36,680 --> 00:12:38,920
Because almost no one wants to.

347
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:40,640
Almost no one on earth wants to come

348
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:44,160
and learn a subject for free, unofficially, off the books.

349
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:45,240
Why not?

350
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:46,760
Well, he's saying, I'll tell you.

351
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:48,000
What about at Princeton?

352
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,760
You know, one of the top schools in the world.

353
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:52,280
They only admit 7% of the applicants

354
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:53,920
who are already really great.

355
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:55,200
So you get turned down by Princeton.

356
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:56,160
Why not just go anyway?

357
00:12:56,160 --> 00:12:58,800
Just move into Princeton, start attending classes.

358
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:02,840
No one's gonna make sure that you're supposed to be there.

359
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:06,040
And in fact, if you do feel, if you're compulsively honest

360
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:07,640
and you go to the professor and say,

361
00:13:07,640 --> 00:13:09,440
I've gotta be honest, I'm not even supposed to be here.

362
00:13:09,440 --> 00:13:12,480
I just love labor economics.

363
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:14,640
Is it all right with you if I study with you

364
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:17,520
and learn at your feet, unofficially?

365
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:21,720
The number of professors who will say, no, get out,

366
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:24,280
it's very small, very small.

367
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:26,400
Normally, professors, when they hear that,

368
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:28,400
get a little tear in their eye.

369
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:31,280
You want to learn what I have to teach

370
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:35,000
for no reason other than a love of the subject.

371
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:36,960
This has never happened before.

372
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:38,560
This is a great day.

373
00:13:38,560 --> 00:13:41,160
All right, that's more the professor's reaction.

374
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:45,200
So why don't people just go to whatever college

375
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:46,960
they think is best in the world,

376
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:51,280
learn all the material, and then you don't have

377
00:13:51,280 --> 00:13:52,960
to do enrollment, you don't have to pay any tuition.

378
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:53,800
Why not?

379
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:57,680
You don't get, yeah, there's no official record

380
00:13:57,680 --> 00:13:59,040
you were ever there.

381
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:00,120
Now, sometimes people will say, well,

382
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:01,560
you don't get the feedback from the professor,

383
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:04,320
you don't get to interact with other students.

384
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:08,760
If you were, like, look, you can save $200,000.

385
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:10,640
I think you could meet some other students

386
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:12,240
to talk to, I mean, like, who do they care

387
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:13,080
if you're not officially there?

388
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:14,480
You don't even have to tell them.

389
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:17,520
And then, like, even, you could just tell the professor

390
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:19,120
and he'll probably grade your work anyway.

391
00:14:19,120 --> 00:14:20,080
It's like, what does it cost me?

392
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:22,400
A few minutes, a few minutes, and it means so much

393
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,840
for this one strange lover of learning.

394
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:29,640
And even there, you know, or how about

395
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:30,680
you just don't tell the professor.

396
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:32,560
Whenever the professor says, you're not supposed

397
00:14:32,560 --> 00:14:34,600
to be here, it's just a mix-up of the registrar.

398
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:35,520
Mix-up of the registrar.

399
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:38,360
Like, what professor's gonna go and pick up a phone

400
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:43,000
and start saying, I have a question about student 10013721?

401
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,120
He says that he's, you know, like, he claims

402
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:49,120
that he's supposed to be here, but nothing ever changes

403
00:14:49,120 --> 00:14:50,400
no matter what I do.

404
00:14:50,400 --> 00:14:51,760
What's going on?

405
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:54,120
Right, so no, no, professors don't have time for that.

406
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:55,240
They don't care.

407
00:14:55,240 --> 00:14:57,800
So I'd say you could get an education

408
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:01,000
in every sense of the word if you really were willing

409
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,880
to go and just put in a little extra effort,

410
00:15:03,880 --> 00:15:06,480
and yet almost no one wants to, right?

411
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:09,840
Now, this doesn't mean that the, you know,

412
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:11,560
so, well, let me just say, like, a thought experiment

413
00:15:11,560 --> 00:15:14,840
I like is, would you rather have the Princeton diploma

414
00:15:14,840 --> 00:15:17,040
without the education, or the Princeton education

415
00:15:17,040 --> 00:15:17,880
with no diploma?

416
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:21,600
Now, I'm not sure which answer is right,

417
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:24,680
but just the fact that you have to think about it

418
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:26,640
says a lot.

419
00:15:26,640 --> 00:15:30,640
It's not a no-brainer, as human capital theory

420
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:32,800
would say that it is, right?

421
00:15:32,800 --> 00:15:34,080
I think I already mentioned how, you know,

422
00:15:34,080 --> 00:15:35,640
here's another fun one.

423
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:39,040
Why is it that students so eagerly seek out easy graders?

424
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:42,760
Yes, because employers don't know

425
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:44,640
that you didn't work very hard for the grade.

426
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:46,600
Now, they do know if you're in a hard major or not.

427
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:48,600
That's one that people can guess at,

428
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:51,280
and also some classes sound easier than others.

429
00:15:51,280 --> 00:15:53,240
So real analysis sounds harder

430
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:55,080
than underwater basket weaving,

431
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:57,920
but once you've picked your major and your subject,

432
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:00,120
then you want to find the super easy person.

433
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:02,880
Now, what about the possibility

434
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:05,160
that the easy person won't teach you very much?

435
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:06,640
That seems totally reasonable,

436
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:08,160
and yet how many people decide

437
00:16:08,160 --> 00:16:09,800
to go take the harder grade for fear

438
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:13,320
that they will miss out in terms of human capital creation?

439
00:16:13,320 --> 00:16:14,640
Very rare.

440
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:18,600
All right, so that is a quick version of my book,

441
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:20,400
The Case Understanding Against Education.

442
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:21,640
There's tons more.

443
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:23,960
I obsessively read because I don't like writing

444
00:16:23,960 --> 00:16:25,760
about a subject when I don't feel really knowledgeable

445
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:28,000
about it, and education is so complicated,

446
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:29,560
and also it's so thoroughly researched,

447
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:30,440
there's just a lot to read,

448
00:16:30,440 --> 00:16:34,120
because so many people are interested in education.

449
00:16:34,120 --> 00:16:35,200
First of all, it's a huge industry,

450
00:16:35,200 --> 00:16:36,120
and second of all, of course,

451
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:37,400
academics are navel gazers.

452
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:39,600
We're obsessed with our own industry.

453
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:41,320
So, all right.

454
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:44,200
Now, just some other examples.

455
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:45,520
So, healthcare.

456
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:48,440
Robin Hansen has a well-known paper

457
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:49,480
called Showing That You Care,

458
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:51,280
The Evolution of Health Altruism,

459
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:52,840
where he argues that a lot of healthcare

460
00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:54,360
is actually about signaling.

461
00:16:54,360 --> 00:16:55,320
This is one where I first heard it,

462
00:16:55,320 --> 00:16:57,160
I thought he was crazy.

463
00:16:57,160 --> 00:17:00,120
But over the time, he has won me over more and more.

464
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:03,560
So, one thing that he told me that I had never heard before

465
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:05,560
is that when empirical health economists

466
00:17:05,560 --> 00:17:08,680
estimate the effect of medical care on health,

467
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:10,120
they find a much smaller effect

468
00:17:10,120 --> 00:17:11,760
than most people would expect.

469
00:17:11,760 --> 00:17:13,040
Much smaller.

470
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:14,240
So, we can sort of have the whole idea

471
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:16,640
that most of the rise in life expectancy

472
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:18,520
is due to improving medical care.

473
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:20,520
No one who knows the data believes that.

474
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:24,560
Which I just took for granted until he told me that.

475
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:26,840
So, there's that.

476
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:30,160
Just in terms of the actual payoff for health,

477
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:31,000
it's overrated.

478
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:34,400
So, that's just getting started.

479
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:37,560
But then he started pointing out how often people

480
00:17:37,560 --> 00:17:39,240
will go and pressure you to get healthcare

481
00:17:39,240 --> 00:17:41,840
even when medical science says

482
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:43,680
there's almost nothing healthcare can do for you.

483
00:17:43,680 --> 00:17:45,520
Like if you have a virus,

484
00:17:45,520 --> 00:17:47,680
what can a doctor actually do for you?

485
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:50,560
Like, give me antibiotics for my viruses.

486
00:17:50,560 --> 00:17:53,680
So, well, they can mess up your antibiotic resistance.

487
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:55,160
That's what they can do for you.

488
00:17:55,160 --> 00:17:56,920
So, yeah, so there's a lot of conditions

489
00:17:56,920 --> 00:17:58,880
that doctors can't do much for.

490
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:00,600
You may even notice when you go to doctors

491
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:02,600
how rarely they can actually solve your problem

492
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:04,320
or do anything for you.

493
00:18:04,320 --> 00:18:05,280
At least that's my experience.

494
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:08,400
I've had a bunch of different weird medical problems.

495
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:11,080
Nothing that serious, but like all in the vicinity

496
00:18:11,080 --> 00:18:13,640
of unpleasant aches and pains.

497
00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:17,080
And I have never had a doctor who has any help at all,

498
00:18:17,080 --> 00:18:19,080
really, for any of this stuff.

499
00:18:19,080 --> 00:18:21,040
You know, like, they're just useless.

500
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:27,160
For back pain, knee pain, foot pain, neck pain, right?

501
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:31,280
And so, in the end, what I did is,

502
00:18:31,280 --> 00:18:34,440
well, so whenever there were any products available

503
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:35,280
at the pharmacy, I would just say,

504
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:37,520
I'm gonna buy every possible foot orthotic,

505
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:40,960
everyone, and try them all and see if anything works.

506
00:18:40,960 --> 00:18:44,520
So that was very useful and cost less than one doctor visit

507
00:18:44,520 --> 00:18:48,080
to buy every single foot orthotic in the entire store.

508
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:49,400
Well, not like the entire stock,

509
00:18:49,400 --> 00:18:51,760
but like one of every product that was being offered.

510
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:53,800
And that did way more for me.

511
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:58,760
So, but then, Robin points out that health insurance

512
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:02,320
often seems to be part of some kind of relationship

513
00:19:02,320 --> 00:19:06,400
where if you don't provide it, you're considered to be mean.

514
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:07,800
So like an employer doesn't provide healthcare,

515
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:10,000
you think they don't care about their employees.

516
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:11,840
If a parent doesn't take to the kid at the doctor,

517
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:13,480
they're a bad parent.

518
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:15,880
So again, it seems, and it does seem like people

519
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:18,040
have some sense of it would be embarrassing.

520
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:21,080
It would be shameful to them if they didn't provide it.

521
00:19:22,040 --> 00:19:23,960
Now then, well, I mean, I thought more about this

522
00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:24,960
when I actually went to the doctor,

523
00:19:24,960 --> 00:19:26,880
noticed all the propaganda there.

524
00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:28,720
And the propaganda is so much about caring

525
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:33,240
and loving people, not about numerous things like,

526
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:36,680
73% of the people who come with this problem can be cured

527
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:40,080
versus only 20% who go in the placebo group.

528
00:19:40,080 --> 00:19:41,960
There's no propaganda like that.

529
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:43,840
It's always like, I love the doctor,

530
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:45,920
the doctor cares about me.

531
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:48,000
And like, even for stuff that it seems like

